    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "24 to 48 hours seems like a massive window for just a simple record update. is it actually traveling through every server manually or is it just a matter of the cache expiring? i'm worried something is actually blocking the update from going through.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Yes, DNS propagation primarily depends on caching rather than servers manually 'travelling' through information. When you update an A record, it means you change the IP address to which your domain name points. That new record doesn't instantaneously update across the entire internet; instead, it's stored momentarily at each DNS server. Clients further away may still request the record directly from their own local cache past the TTL setting. This is why you might see a delay of up to 48 hours. It's normal behavior and usually not a cause for concern as the updates eventually spread throughout the system. However, if this delay seems excessive, it's always a good idea to verify that the record update was successful and to ensure there's no technical issue with the client's side.",
